1
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:06,380
Hello, I am Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:09,900
Welcome to Sheldon Cooper Presents: Fun with Flags.

3
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:14,020
Before we get started, I'd like to announce

4
00:00:14,070 --> 00:00:16,940
the winner of our design your own flag competition.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:18,470
But I can't.

6
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:21,640
The only entry was from GameyGamer75,

7
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:24,000
and I know that was a jpeg of your buttocks.

8
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:28,360
Now this week we have a very special episode

9
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:29,800
where we explore the flags

10
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,000
of the popular entertainment franchise, Star Trek.

11
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,420
And to help me, I'm pleased to introduce

12
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:37,140
Internet personality,

13
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:40,060
former star of Star Trek: The Next Generation,

14
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:43,630
and the only guy I know lucky enough to be immortalized

15
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:45,260
in 1/16 scale--

16
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:47,850
set phasers to "Fun"...

17
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:52,940
...for my friend, Wil Wheaton.

18
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,370
Hi, Sheldon. Thanks for having me.

19
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:58,360
- I'm happy to be here. - Cut.

20
00:00:59,230 --> 00:01:00,340
What's wrong?

21
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:02,940
Sorry, Sheldon, you were brilliant as always.

22
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:05,750
Wil, that was a little wooden.

23
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:07,700
Wooden?

24
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,250
Don't worry, it wasn't terrible.

25
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:13,370
Just, this time, try to say it the way people sound.

26
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:17,170
And action.

27
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,100
My friend, Wil Wheaton.

28
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:20,390
Hi, Sheldon. Thanks for having me.

29
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:21,580
I'm excited to be here.

30
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:24,370
So, Wil, what do you have for us first?

31
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:26,180
Well, this is an exciting one.

32
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:28,710
This is the flag of the United Federation of Planets.

33
00:01:28,710 --> 00:01:30,750
- Now what's interesting about this flag... - Cut.

34
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:33,760
What was wrong with that?

35
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:36,600
It's called "Fun with Flags."

36
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,550
They're not at half-mast, nobody died.

37
00:01:38,550 --> 00:01:40,210
Let's try and keep it upbeat.

38
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:43,340
Um, no offense,

39
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:45,020
but I've been acting since I was a kid.

40
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:46,440
I think I can handle a Web show

41
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:47,670
without a lot of direction.

42
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:48,730
It's true.

43
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:50,810
In 1982, Wil played the voice

44
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:53,400
of Martin the mouse in The Secret of NIMH.

45
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:54,930
You moved me.

46
00:01:55,950 --> 00:01:57,730
You'll have to forgive me.

47
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:00,810
This is my first time directing, I just want it to be good.

48
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:02,270
- So do I. - Great.

49
00:02:02,270 --> 00:02:03,710
So, this time let's try

50
00:02:03,710 --> 00:02:05,940
more real boy, less Pinocchio.

51
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:08,640
And action.

52
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,060
And cut.

53
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:13,170
You realize that I'm doing this for free, right?

54
00:02:13,170 --> 00:02:17,340
Yes. And so far, we're still not getting our money's worth.

55
00:02:19,590 --> 00:02:20,880
Let's try it again.

56
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:23,580
Everybody's having fun.

57
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:26,060
And action.

58
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:27,620
So, Wil...

59
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:38,290
...what do you have for us first?

60
00:02:38,290 --> 00:02:41,850
Well, this is an exciting one.

61
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:43,350
This is the flag

62
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:45,520
of the United Federation of Planets.

63
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:46,700
Cut.

64
00:02:47,510 --> 00:02:50,370
Problem, first-time director?

65
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:51,700
Oh, none that I could see.

66
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:54,630
I saw a man who loved flags almost as much as I do.

67
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:56,660
I got goose bumps.

68
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,240
He was overacting on purpose.

69
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:00,160
Really?

70
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,910
He reminded me of a young William Shatner.

71
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:06,030
Listen, Sheldon, I'm really happy to do this for you,

72
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:09,380
but not if she's gonna be a huge pain in the ass the whole time.

73
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,120
You gonna let him speak to me like that?

74
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:14,720
Well...

75
00:03:16,430 --> 00:03:17,910
you're my girlfriend

76
00:03:17,910 --> 00:03:19,920
and I don't want you to be upset.

77
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,800
Then again, Wil Wheaton's my friend

78
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:24,110
and I don't want him to be upset.

79
00:03:25,530 --> 00:03:27,200
This is a sticky wicket.

80
00:03:28,690 --> 00:03:30,070
What do you think?

81
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:32,920
Can I speak to you for a second?

82
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:34,710
I'll be right back.

83
00:03:35,090 --> 00:03:37,170
Feel free to play with yourself.

84
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:45,620
I don't care for your friend,

85
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:47,220
he's being rude to me.

86
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:48,560
You need to ask him to leave.

87
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,550
Amy, I can't just ask Wil Wheaton to leave.

88
00:03:51,550 --> 00:03:53,570
He's a minor celebrity.

89
00:03:54,230 --> 00:03:57,350
Once you explain who he is, many people recognize him.

90
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:00,440
Fine. Then maybe I should go.

91
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:02,880
Could you? That would solve everything.

92
00:04:04,630 --> 00:04:06,650
You are the best.

93
00:04:06,650 --> 00:04:08,590
I'll see you at dinner tonight?

94
00:04:08,590 --> 00:04:09,820
You sure you wouldn't rather have dinner

95
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:11,050
with your friend Wil Wheaton?

96
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:13,050
Come to think of it, I would!

97
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:17,240
You, little lady, are on fire.

98
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:45,870
Every time we eat dinner here,

99
00:04:45,870 --> 00:04:48,230
your mother refuses to let me help with the dishes.

100
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:51,010
Don't take it personally. She likes doing them by herself

101
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:53,850
so she can lick the plates with no one looking.

102
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:55,670
You ready to go?

103
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:59,820
Yeah, let me just grab a couple of fresh turtlenecks.

104
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:01,820
I don't understand why you keep your stuff here

105
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:03,620
when there's plenty of room at home.

106
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:05,280
What are you talking about?

107
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:08,210
All I have here is a few sweaters,

108
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:10,180
books, bank stuff, computers,

109
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:13,250
mail, collectibles, medicine and my electric body groomer.

110
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:17,640
Ooh, there's my plaid dickie.

111
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:20,710
Oh, got this at the Goodwill store for 50 cents.

112
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:21,630
Can you believe it?

113
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:26,100
50 cents sounds right.

114
00:05:28,490 --> 00:05:29,630
Let's go.

115
00:05:29,690 --> 00:05:30,890
You know, it's kinda late.

116
00:05:30,890 --> 00:05:32,490
Why don't we just spend the night here?

117
00:05:32,490 --> 00:05:34,090
Because we don't live here.

118
00:05:34,090 --> 00:05:35,360
I know.

119
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:37,360
Do you? You said when you got back from space

120
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:38,890
you were gonna move into my apartment,

121
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:40,530
but half the time we stay here.

122
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:42,800
That's not true.

123
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,710
Howard, I'm doing laundry!

124
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:47,100
You want me to put anything in for you?!

125
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:50,030
There's some underwear in the hamper!

126
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:54,500
Oh, good! I got that new stain stick to try out!

127
00:05:56,430 --> 00:05:59,820
Thank you! I only put it on the list two weeks ago!

128
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:03,680
Okay, I see what you're getting at.

129
00:06:04,870 --> 00:06:06,700
How about this weekend

130
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:11,820
I'll box up all my things and move them to our place.

131
00:06:11,820 --> 00:06:13,320
Thank you.

132
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:15,960
The lightsabers are gonna look great in the living room.

133
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:18,330
Or in the closet. We can decide later.

134
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:21,090
Howard, help!

135
00:06:21,090 --> 00:06:24,390
My hand's stuck in the garbage disposal!

136
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:28,130
Let go of whatever piece of food you're holding!

137
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:29,700
Are you kidding?!

138
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:32,880
It's a perfectly good chicken leg!

139
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:38,510
Hey, look who's out after dark, like a big boy.

140
00:06:39,370 --> 00:06:42,300
I was out raising heck with Mr. Wil Wheaton.

141
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:43,620
Four hours more and we would have

142
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:45,800
closed down the HomeTown Buffet.

143
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:49,130
- I thought you had plans with Amy. - Yeah, I did,

144
00:06:49,130 --> 00:06:53,020
but then Wil called Amy a pain in the A-S-S.

145
00:06:53,690 --> 00:06:54,960
She got huffy and left,

146
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:56,780
then Wil and I headed out to dinner.

147
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:00,100
That place really did remind me of my hometown.

148
00:07:01,070 --> 00:07:04,310
Because there we also have a HomeTown Buffet.

149
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:08,070
Hold on. Wil and Amy had an argument?

150
00:07:08,070 --> 00:07:10,300
Yes, quite the kerfuffle.

151
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:12,320
Then Amy got mad and left?

152
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:14,090
Walked right out the door.

153
00:07:14,090 --> 00:07:15,460
And you...?

154
00:07:15,990 --> 00:07:18,700
Enjoyed a delightful dinner at a reasonable price.

155
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:21,710
The manager recognized Wil

156
00:07:21,710 --> 00:07:24,400
and let us sit right next to the frozen yogurt machine.

157
00:07:24,790 --> 00:07:26,220
Right next to it!

158
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:29,170
I was closer to it than I am to you right now.

159
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,360
Buddy, I think Amy might be upset.

160
00:07:34,410 --> 00:07:35,710
Why's that?

161
00:07:35,710 --> 00:07:37,490
Because your friend was rude to her,

162
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:39,700
and then you went to dinner with him.

163
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:41,820
You're just repeating what I said.

164
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:45,200
It's like living with a lactose-intolerant parrot.

165
00:07:47,630 --> 00:07:49,610
Trust me, call her.

166
00:07:50,090 --> 00:07:53,640
Fine. It's a shame you didn't go to dinner with us,

167
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:55,130
because the buffet you're about to enjoy

168
00:07:55,130 --> 00:07:56,930
only serves humble pie,

169
00:07:56,930 --> 00:07:59,620
a dessert much less tasty than frozen yogurt.

170
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:01,400
I was this close.

171
00:08:04,950 --> 00:08:06,060
What?

172
00:08:07,290 --> 00:08:08,710
You'll appreciate this.

173
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:11,540
Leonard has some ridiculous notion that you're mad at me.

174
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:13,310
Tell him you're not mad at me.

175
00:08:13,310 --> 00:08:14,680
Go ahead, set him straight.

176
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:16,670
I'm mad at you, Sheldon.

177
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:20,860
Eat one of your Luna bars.

178
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:23,890
Very often when women think they're angry,

179
00:08:23,890 --> 00:08:25,680
they're really just hungry.

180
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:29,480
I'm not hungry.

181
00:08:30,010 --> 00:08:32,330
Your friend insulted me, and you didn't do anything.

182
00:08:32,330 --> 00:08:35,590
Precisely, I didn't do anything.

183
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:40,000
Now does someone feel like checking her emotional math?

184
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:42,910
Keep going, buddy, you're doing great.

185
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:45,230
Sheldon, I'm your girlfriend,

186
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:47,070
and you should have taken my side.

187
00:08:47,070 --> 00:08:50,300
That's it. End of story. Good night.

188
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:56,800
Wow, Amy's mad and Leonard was right.

189
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:58,710
What a weird day.

190
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:03,290
Hey. Sorry this took so long.

191
00:09:03,290 --> 00:09:05,280
But you used to work here, you know how it is.

192
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:06,580
Kitchen slammed again?

193
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:08,970
No, I'm a terrible waitress, remember?

194
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:11,840
So, is there anything I can do

195
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:13,400
to help you with the move tomorrow?

196
00:09:13,410 --> 00:09:14,740
Now that you mention it,

197
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:17,850
I was thinking tomorrow might not be great.

198
00:09:17,850 --> 00:09:21,200
- What's your excuse this time? - No excuse.

199
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:23,580
It's just, you know, I'm Jewish, and technically,

200
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:26,420
we're not supposed to drive or carry anything on the Sabbath.

201
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:28,380
So this one's on God.

202
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,030
That might be a little more convincing

203
00:09:31,030 --> 00:09:34,590
if you didn't have a mouthful of bacon cheeseburger.

204
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:37,440
My religion's kinda loosey-goosey.

205
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:39,510
Basically, as long as you got your schmekel clipped

206
00:09:39,510 --> 00:09:41,540
and don't wear a cross, you're good.

207
00:09:43,910 --> 00:09:45,470
Howie, you promised you'd move.

208
00:09:45,470 --> 00:09:46,550
And I will.

209
00:09:46,550 --> 00:09:48,270
- Yeah, right. - I will.

210
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:50,510
I'm obviously not going to live in my mother's house

211
00:09:50,510 --> 00:09:53,320
for the rest of my life. I'm not a child.

212
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:55,550
I've seen her burp you.

213
00:09:58,740 --> 00:09:59,720
She did not burp me.

214
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:02,780
She was patting me on the back, and I happened to burp.

215
00:10:06,010 --> 00:10:07,990
Don't you have other tables you should be waiting on?

216
00:10:07,990 --> 00:10:10,930
Yeah, but I told you, I'm not good at my job.

217
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:12,280
Bernadette, listen to me.

218
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:13,610
He is never gonna leave.

219
00:10:13,610 --> 00:10:15,190
I'm starting to think you're right.

220
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:16,910
All right, I've had enough of this.

221
00:10:16,910 --> 00:10:19,470
I'm a grown man, I have a successful career.

222
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:21,980
For the love of God, I've been to space!

223
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:23,680
I will move out when I'm ready,

224
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,150
and I don't need anyone badgering me into it!

225
00:10:26,650 --> 00:10:28,930
Wow, excuse me.

226
00:10:30,860 --> 00:10:32,010
That was just for her benefit.

227
00:10:32,010 --> 00:10:34,310
I'll move tomorrow. I love you. Don't leave me.

228
00:10:39,080 --> 00:10:39,960
Amy.

229
00:10:40,670 --> 00:10:41,770
Amy.

230
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:44,150
Angry Amy.

231
00:10:46,490 --> 00:10:47,690
What?

232
00:10:47,690 --> 00:10:49,960
I've been thinking about what happened,

233
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:52,250
and I hope this gift

234
00:10:52,250 --> 00:10:54,090
will make things better.

235
00:10:56,540 --> 00:10:58,960
Star Trek DVDs?

236
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:01,190
Why would I want this?

237
00:11:01,190 --> 00:11:03,030
First of all, you're welcome.

238
00:11:03,830 --> 00:11:07,300
Furthermore, not being familiar with Wil Wheaton's body of work

239
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:08,550
there was no way for you to know

240
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:11,080
you were being rude to a national treasure.

241
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:15,180
Get ready for 130 hours of "I told you so."

242
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:22,380
Fine. I'll just tell you what happens.

243
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:26,510
Episode one, "Encounter at Farpoint."

244
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:27,770
Fade in.

245
00:11:27,770 --> 00:11:30,550
The new Enterprise heads out on its maiden voyage

246
00:11:30,550 --> 00:11:33,210
to contact the Bandi people of Deneb IV.

247
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:36,120
Enter Wesley Crusher, played by my buddy...

248
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:42,820
She's hooked.

249
00:11:52,370 --> 00:11:54,250
An end of an era.

250
00:11:54,710 --> 00:11:58,120
Boy, if these walls could talk.

251
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:02,230
They'd say, "Why does he touch himself so much?"

252
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:08,800
Yeah.

253
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:14,760
I can't believe I'm not going to live here anymore.

254
00:12:14,830 --> 00:12:18,300
This has always been my bedroom.

255
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:21,600
Right here is where my mom used to mark my height.

256
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:23,300
Oh, yeah.

257
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:24,770
Fifth grade.

258
00:12:24,770 --> 00:12:26,200
Sixth grade.

259
00:12:26,210 --> 00:12:27,470
Seventh grade.

260
00:12:27,470 --> 00:12:28,610
Eighth grade.

261
00:12:28,610 --> 00:12:30,570
Ninth grade.

262
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:34,480
I remember when I was five,

263
00:12:34,510 --> 00:12:38,450
hiding under this desk with all my Halloween candy.

264
00:12:38,450 --> 00:12:39,880
Had some Peanut M&M'S,

265
00:12:40,780 --> 00:12:43,080
went into my first anaphylactic shock

266
00:12:44,660 --> 00:12:46,250
and had to be rushed to the hospital.

267
00:12:46,250 --> 00:12:48,200
Came home, celebrated with a Snickers,

268
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:50,750
went into my second anaphylactic shock.

269
00:12:52,230 --> 00:12:54,410
When did you figure out you were allergic to nuts?

270
00:12:54,410 --> 00:12:56,800
Sometime around the third Almond Joy.

271
00:12:57,750 --> 00:12:59,120
Okay.

272
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:01,070
You want to start loading this stuff into the truck?

273
00:13:01,070 --> 00:13:03,110
Yeah, I guess.

274
00:13:03,110 --> 00:13:05,140
Hey, would you do me a favor?

275
00:13:05,140 --> 00:13:06,060
Go on ahead.

276
00:13:06,060 --> 00:13:10,680
I just want one last moment alone in my old room.

277
00:13:10,680 --> 00:13:12,640
We're not standing outside by the U-Haul

278
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:14,770
while you fondle yourself.

279
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:19,180
Fine, let's go.

280
00:13:26,550 --> 00:13:28,300
What brings you in?

281
00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:30,040
Penny, if you wouldn't mind,

282
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:33,370
I'd like to have a conversation about girls.

283
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:37,800
I had a feeling we'd have a talk like this sooner or later.

284
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:42,040
Are you finally getting fuzz in weird places?

285
00:13:43,020 --> 00:13:44,250
Penny, please,

286
00:13:44,250 --> 00:13:46,930
I'm on the horns of a relationship dilemma.

287
00:13:46,930 --> 00:13:48,560
And for the record, I had a full pubis of hair

288
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:50,590
by the time I was 19.

289
00:13:52,810 --> 00:13:53,570
And for the record...

290
00:13:56,840 --> 00:13:58,510
So what are you drinking?

291
00:13:58,510 --> 00:13:59,960
Well, it's been a rough day.

292
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:01,580
I usually go chamomile tea,

293
00:14:01,580 --> 00:14:03,480
but I don't think that's going to cut it.

294
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:06,390
You could have a Long Island Iced Tea.

295
00:14:09,830 --> 00:14:12,050
Will that calm my nerves?

296
00:14:13,020 --> 00:14:16,060
It's calmed the pants off me a couple of times.

297
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:18,200
Sold.

298
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:23,690
So, the heart you got from the wizard giving you trouble?

299
00:14:24,490 --> 00:14:26,150
The trouble isn't with me, Penny,

300
00:14:26,150 --> 00:14:27,690
it's with your gender.

301
00:14:27,730 --> 00:14:29,130
Someday, scientists will discover

302
00:14:29,130 --> 00:14:31,100
that second X chromosome contains nothing

303
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:33,230
but nonsense and twaddle.

304
00:14:34,340 --> 00:14:36,240
Yeah, Amy told me what happened.

305
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:37,480
Look, just apologize.

306
00:14:37,480 --> 00:14:39,150
It'll warm her twaddle.

307
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:42,330
It's a band-aid at best.

308
00:14:42,330 --> 00:14:46,100
The core problem is that Amy and Wil do not like each other.

309
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:49,830
Which is baffling because they're both crazy about me.

310
00:14:50,830 --> 00:14:51,800
And I like them,

311
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:54,360
which indicates they're bright and interesting

312
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,530
and/or were on Star Trek.

313
00:14:57,630 --> 00:15:00,230
Honey, you can't make people like each other.

314
00:15:00,230 --> 00:15:01,530
Not true.

315
00:15:01,530 --> 00:15:03,390
Leonard made me like you.

316
00:15:03,390 --> 00:15:05,780
And let me tell you, that was a hard row to hoe.

317
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:08,750
Cheers, pal.

318
00:15:13,010 --> 00:15:16,610
Boy, that is a treat that's hard to beat.

319
00:15:17,340 --> 00:15:21,020
Get the Mad Hatter on the horn, I'm having a tea party.

320
00:15:26,570 --> 00:15:28,620
You might want to pace yourself.

321
00:15:28,620 --> 00:15:30,430
I drink tea all the time.

322
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:31,990
I think I know what I'm doing.

323
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:36,680
Far be it from me to criticize a man with a full pubis.

324
00:15:39,110 --> 00:15:41,310
Look, Sheldon, your problem is not Wil Wheaton, okay?

325
00:15:41,310 --> 00:15:43,580
Your problem is the way you treated Amy.

326
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:45,550
My problem is I'm out of tea.

327
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:49,640
Come on, someone insulted your girlfriend

328
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:50,970
and you just let him do it.

329
00:15:50,970 --> 00:15:54,100
I thought you Texas guys stood up for your womenfolk.

330
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:55,660
Penny, please,

331
00:15:55,660 --> 00:15:59,030
I think I've evolved beyond my simple rustic upbringing.

332
00:15:59,030 --> 00:16:00,080
Sorry.

333
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:01,380
On the other hand,

334
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:04,190
that low-down polecat done wronged my woman.

335
00:16:07,410 --> 00:16:09,470
Welcome to Long Island, Tex.

336
00:16:11,300 --> 00:16:13,500
Amy deserves better.

337
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,680
You know, when we buy the Planter's deluxe mix,

338
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:17,570
she eats all the Brazil nuts

339
00:16:17,570 --> 00:16:19,360
so I don't have to look at them.

340
00:16:20,180 --> 00:16:23,530
She's a unique blend of saint and squirrel.

341
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:25,970
Yeah, that she is.

342
00:16:25,970 --> 00:16:26,870
Here you go.

343
00:16:26,870 --> 00:16:28,990
I'm a callous egomaniac.

344
00:16:28,990 --> 00:16:30,420
She's gonna leave me.

345
00:16:30,450 --> 00:16:31,580
No, she won't.

346
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:33,600
No, she won't. I'm great.

347
00:16:45,590 --> 00:16:47,410
Okay, I have now

348
00:16:47,410 --> 00:16:50,120
officially moved out of my mother's house.

349
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:51,980
You are now the only woman in my life

350
00:16:51,980 --> 00:16:53,920
who I'll see naked in the bathroom.

351
00:16:55,180 --> 00:16:56,760
I know this wasn't easy.

352
00:16:56,760 --> 00:16:58,040
You doing okay?

353
00:16:58,040 --> 00:16:59,240
Oh, I'm fine.

354
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:01,550
It's just her I'm worried about.

355
00:17:01,550 --> 00:17:03,090
Aw, she'll be okay.

356
00:17:03,090 --> 00:17:05,020
- She's a grown woman. - I know.

357
00:17:05,020 --> 00:17:07,090
It's just ever since my dad left,

358
00:17:07,090 --> 00:17:09,620
I've felt responsible for her.

359
00:17:09,620 --> 00:17:12,160
That's a lot for a kid to deal with.

360
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:15,290
She was just so sad all the time.

361
00:17:15,290 --> 00:17:18,610
Yeah, I was the only person who could cheer her up.

362
00:17:18,610 --> 00:17:21,100
Well, me and Ben and Jerry.

363
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:24,020
She's lucky you were there.

364
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:28,870
You know, she's why I first got into magic.

365
00:17:29,530 --> 00:17:31,130
I would do little shows for her,

366
00:17:31,130 --> 00:17:34,750
hang up a bedsheet like it was a curtain,

367
00:17:34,750 --> 00:17:36,630
put on a top hat and cape.

368
00:17:36,630 --> 00:17:38,740
And part way through the act,

369
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:42,420
I would say I needed a volunteer from the audience

370
00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:45,220
to be my beautiful assistant

371
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:47,990
and invite her up on stage.

372
00:17:47,990 --> 00:17:51,730
I can still remember the way she'd smile.

373
00:17:52,610 --> 00:17:54,330
For a few minutes, she'd...

374
00:17:54,330 --> 00:17:57,400
forget how lonely she was.

375
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:01,550
Aw, crap. Let's go.

376
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:04,920
Where we going?

377
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:05,790
Grab a box.

378
00:18:05,790 --> 00:18:07,610
We'll sleep at your mother's place tonight.

379
00:18:07,610 --> 00:18:09,660
No, but I want to live here.

380
00:18:09,660 --> 00:18:10,730
Well, you should've thought of that

381
00:18:10,730 --> 00:18:14,170
before you told me the stupid magic trick story.

382
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:16,460
Can't we talk about this?

383
00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:20,150
No husband of mine is gonna break his mother's heart!

384
00:18:27,510 --> 00:18:28,690
Wil Wheaton!

385
00:18:29,290 --> 00:18:30,970
Wil Wheaton!

386
00:18:32,170 --> 00:18:34,320
Wait, how many was that?

387
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:38,530
Hey, Sheldon, what's up?

388
00:18:38,530 --> 00:18:40,870
Wouldn't you like to know?

389
00:18:42,510 --> 00:18:43,680
Have you been drinking?

390
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:45,200
Just tea.

391
00:18:46,490 --> 00:18:48,920
The best tea I've ever had.

392
00:18:50,830 --> 00:18:52,470
Why are you here?

393
00:18:52,470 --> 00:18:54,220
I'll tell you.

394
00:18:54,220 --> 00:18:56,560
I'm from Texas.

395
00:18:57,080 --> 00:18:59,030
Need I say more?

396
00:19:00,620 --> 00:19:03,660
Yeah, actually, a little more would be helpful.

397
00:19:03,660 --> 00:19:05,470
You insulted my woman.

398
00:19:05,470 --> 00:19:07,840
I'm here to defend her honor.

399
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:10,070
Two! It was two.

400
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:12,030
Wil Wheaton!

401
00:19:14,890 --> 00:19:18,520
Now prepare yourself for what may come.

402
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:24,050
Oh, Sheldon, do you really think we're gonna fight?

403
00:19:24,090 --> 00:19:25,200
My fists are not up here

404
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:27,980
because I'm milking a giant invisible cow.

405
00:19:30,350 --> 00:19:33,710
They're up to beat an apology out of you.

406
00:19:33,710 --> 00:19:36,290
Okay, I'm sorry.

407
00:19:36,290 --> 00:19:39,330
Well, that was a long bus ride for not very much.

408
00:19:40,620 --> 00:19:41,800
Are you okay?

409
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:44,270
You're asking a lot of questions, Wil Wheaton.

410
00:19:44,810 --> 00:19:46,550
As a matter of idle curiosity,

411
00:19:46,550 --> 00:19:48,590
which of your shrubberies do you feel

412
00:19:48,590 --> 00:19:51,060
would benefit from a thorough vomiting?

413
00:19:51,780 --> 00:19:53,170
Never mind, I'll choose.

414
00:19:59,660 --> 00:20:02,350
You were so good in Stand by Me.

415
00:20:10,790 --> 00:20:13,780
Hello, I'm Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

416
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:17,200
Welcome to Sheldon Cooper Presents: Fun with Flags.

417
00:20:19,010 --> 00:20:21,020
Get ready for a very special episode

418
00:20:21,020 --> 00:20:22,260
where we explore the flags

419
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:24,610
of the popular entertainment franchise, Star Trek.

420
00:20:25,350 --> 00:20:28,660
And to help us, I'm pleased to introduce a special guest--

421
00:20:28,660 --> 00:20:30,700
surprisingly, it only took gas money

422
00:20:30,700 --> 00:20:33,230
and the promise of free food to get him here--

423
00:20:33,230 --> 00:20:35,460
Mr. LeVar Burton.

424
00:20:37,150 --> 00:20:39,030
Hey, Sheldon, it's a pleasure to be here.

425
00:20:39,030 --> 00:20:41,170
- Well, we've got some interesting flags for... - Cut!

426
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:46,820
Yikes, this guy is worse than Wil Wheaton.

427
00:20:50,080 --> 00:20:51,970
I don't know what she's talking about,

428
00:20:51,970 --> 00:20:53,690
but I'm obligated to agree with her.

429
00:20:53,690 --> 00:20:54,850
She's my girlfriend.

430
00:20:54,850 --> 00:20:56,520
Ah, I hear you, brother.

431
00:20:57,180 --> 00:20:58,750
I still get lunch, right?

